{
  "note": [
    "The Escalator's fabric (DR-106), cut from the one Kalamontee layout in scripts/fabric-kalamontee.mjs -- regenerate from there, do not hand-edit. It is in that file because it was cut in the same pass as the shuttle station, not because it is near it: canon puts it at the far end of the line, DOWN and WEST to Lawanda Platform, UP and EAST to the Fork (comptwo.zil 51-54).",
    "ESCALATOR.metadata.json v2: the shaft between the two neighbours' own mouths, 22 m of run and 3.0 m across, built from props because its floor is the tread line. Its origin is the tread under its eye, Lawanda x 19, and the eye 1.6 m over it. Every number of the flight is scripts/fabric-lawanda.mjs's, accepted 2026-09-14 and painted in LAWANDA-PLATFORM TURN-069: the comb plate at Lawanda x 5.45 on that floor, 71 treads climbing east at 30 degrees to x 30 (0.346 m goings on 0.200 m risers), a 2.2 m tread band, balustrades at z +/-1.3 topping 0.95 m over the treads, concrete shaft sides at z +/-1.5 and the stepped soffit, lamps every 5 m. That painting already shows the flight to about Lawanda x 19.6, past this eye, so this is a continuation of it.",
    "The soffit is stepped, as the accepted fabric steps it -- 3.405 m over the tread line at each step's west end and 2.83 m at its east, the clearance the accepted package states. ESCALATOR v2's largeObject reads it as a smooth 3.405 m throughout, which would stand 0.58 m over the painting at every step; the accepted art wins.",
    "West, down the slope, Lawanda Platform's own 3.0 x 4.3 mouth 12.7 m off, and through it a wedge of that platform's deck back to about its x -1.1 and nothing else -- not its ceiling, not its far wall, neither berth (a ray through the mouth reaches z +/-5 only 9 m beyond the platform's west wall) and not its sign. The mouth's sill falls below a level 110-degree view and belongs to the DOWN cap. East, up the slope, the Fork's 2.8 x 2.8 mouth 11 m off at +23.4 to +34.4 degrees: its upper volume only -- its ceiling to about its own x 3.9, the top of its pier and the threshold -- and neither branch."
  ],
